This is one of the most common `fetch` surprises: the `fetch` promise only rejects on a **network-level failure** — DNS failure, connection refused, CORS block — not on an HTTP error status. A `500` or `404` response is still a successfully completed HTTP exchange as far as the Fetch API is concerned; the promise resolves normally with `response.ok === false` and `response.status === 500`.

Developers coming from libraries like Axios (which does throw on non-2xx by default) get bitten by this constantly — they write a `.catch()` expecting it to handle server errors and it silently never fires, so the code falls through to whatever happens when you try to `.json()` an error page's HTML body or an empty response.

The fix is an explicit check right after the fetch resolves, before touching the body:

```javascript
const res = await fetch(url);
if (!res.ok) {
  throw new Error(`Request failed: ${res.status}`);
}
const data = await res.json();
```

That manually promotes an HTTP error status into a JS exception your existing `.catch()` or `try/catch` can actually handle. I treat this as a standing checklist item whenever I review a new `fetch` call — `res.ok` gets checked before the body is ever parsed, every time, with no exceptions for 'this endpoint always succeeds.'

**Testing:** whether you know fetch's actual rejection semantics precisely, rather than assuming it behaves like other HTTP clients.
